      en: "Cox's Bazar, located on the southeastern coast of Bangladesh, is world-famous for having the longest unbroken natural sandy beach in the world, stretching over 120 kilometers. It is the premier tourist destination in the country, attracting millions of visitors with its stunning sunrises, sunsets, and turquoise waters. The town's name originates from Captain Hiram Cox, a British East India Company officer. Cox's Bazar is also a major hub for the fishing industry and the production of dried fish (shutki). The region has a unique cultural identity, influenced by the local Rakhine community and their Buddhist traditions, visible in the many colorful pagodas and temples. Nearby attractions like Inani Beach, Himchari National Park, and Saint Martin's Island make it a center for eco-tourism. The town has recently gained international attention as the site of one of the world's largest refugee settlements. Geography K7 — Coastal Tourism and Marine Economy."
